{{also|u-turn}}
==English==
[[Image:Bochum - Alleestraße - Westpark - Jahrhunderthalle (oT) 01 ies.ogv|thumb|300px|A car making a '''U-turn'''.]]

===Alternative forms===
* {{l|en|u-turn}}

===Etymology===
[[U]] + [[turn]], from the similarity to the shape of the letter ''U''.

===Pronunciation===
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-PerfectSoundWhatever-U-turn.wav|a=CA}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|literally}} A [[turn]] in a [[vehicle]] carried out by driving in a [[semicircle]] in order to travel in the [[opposite]] [[direction]].
# {{lb|en|figuratively}} A reversal of [[policy]]; an [[about-face]], a [[backflip]].
#: {{ux|en|The government have done a '''U-turn''' and are now supporting greater European integration instead of opposing it.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en-GB|author=Senay Boztas|title=Frans Timmermans urges European left to unite against right’s climate backlash|work=The Guardian|date=2023-10-10|url=https://www.theguardian.com/world/2023/oct/10/frans-timmermans-european-left-to-unite-right-climate-backlash|issn=0261-3077|passage=Rishi Sunak, the UK prime minister, announced a major '''U-turn''' on the government’s climate commitments last month, pushing back the deadline for selling new petrol and diesel cars and the phasing out of gas boilers.}}

====Usage notes====
* Often used in the phrases {{m|en||do a U-turn}} or {{m|en||make a U-turn}}.

====Synonyms====
* {{l|en|uey}} {{q|colloquial}}

